The equations of lines \(\vec{v}\) and \(\vec{w}\) intersect at a point.  Let \(\vec{v} = \begin{pmatrix} x_1 \\ y_1 \\ z_1 \end{pmatrix}\) and \(\vec{w} = \begin{pmatrix} x_2 \\ y_2 \\ z_2 \end{pmatrix}\).  For the lines to intersect, we must have: \(\begin{pmatrix} x_1 \\ y_1 \\ z_1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
  7 + t(-2) \\ -3 + t(5) \\ 1 + t(1) \end{pmatrix}\) and \(\begin{pmatrix} x_2 \\ y_2 \\ z_2 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
  8 + u(1) \\ -1 + u(-4) \\ -1 + u(0) \end{pmatrix}\)The points at which the two lines intersect must be the same.  Now, let us solve for \(t\) and \(u\) by equating corresponding entries in our vectors.  In particular, \(\begin{cases}
  7+(-2)t = 8+u & \text{(1)} \\ \\ -3 + 5t = -1 - 4u & \text{(2)} \\ \\ 1+t = -1 & \text{(3)}
\end{cases}\).  From (3), \(t = -2\).  Substituting \(t = -2\) into (1) and (2), we obtain \(\begin{cases}
  7+4 = 8+u & \text{(from (1))}\\ \\ -3 - 10 = -1 + 8u & \text{(from (2))}
\end{cases}\), or \(\begin{cases}
  9 = 8 + u & \text{(from (1))}\\ \\-12 = 8u & \text{(from (2))}
\end{cases}\).  Hence, \(u = -\frac{3}{2}\).  Putting \(t = -2\) and \(u = -\frac{3}{2}\) into (1), (2), or (3) then yields the point \((x, y, z) = (11, -13, -1)\).</think> <answer>(11, -13, -1) </answer>

